# Commands run by the launchd job com.redwood.demo (see com.redwood.demo.plist).
# RC_JOBS=rc.jobs.bt1, REST=1 (with WIFID=1), MORE_JOBS="com.apple.bluetoothd com.apple.BlueTool",
# OSLOG="com.apple.backboardd com.apple.bluetoothd" so bluetoothd's own os_log reaches the console,
# BT=1 (build.sh copies bt_test into /redwood/bin and adds libredwood to BlueTool and bluetoothd, so the
# service com.apple.BlueTool is ours), RUNATLOAD="com.apple.BlueTool" (started before it is asked for:
# in run 101 launchd never scheduled it, and bluetoothd's boot script timed out for want of a service),
# connsys_step=17 connsys_bt=2, XNU_SECS=110.
#
# What is in place after runs 89 and 90: the controller answers (WMT's function on, then six HCI
# commands: reset, local version, BD_ADDR (redacted), features, buffers 1021/6, LE 251/8),
# the "bluetooth" node is in the registry, /dev/cu.bluetooth and /dev/uart.bluetooth exist (major 19),
# com.apple.uart.sk.bluetooth and its Skywalk pipe are up, and bluetoothd (pid 21) is running with
# com.apple.server.bluetooth given out. What has not been seen once is a byte of HCI going the other
# way: btsk_sync_tx has never had a slot.
#
# bt_test asks the same questions from an application's side -- the central manager's state,
# MobileGestalt's "bluetooth", bluetoothd's four Mach services, BluetoothManager's attach error (the
# value -[BluetoothManager _attach] keeps), and a 20 s scan for advertisers. If the state is 5 and
# the scan comes back empty, the gap is below CoreBluetooth; if the state stays 0 or 4, it is the
# attach through BluetoothManager that fails, and its error number says where.
/redwood/bin/net_test wait 22
/redwood/bin/net_test dhcp en0 waitaddr en0 10
/redwood/bin/net_test lookup com.apple.server.bluetooth
/redwood/bin/net_test procs bluetooth
/redwood/bin/bt_test 20
/redwood/bin/net_test procs bluetooth
/redwood/bin/net_test end